      "Language": "dut",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 50-year-old nurse with chronic back pain developed painful paraesthesia in the legs and saddle region during walking. Because pain in the legs could not be provoked by standing erect, the orthotic-lordotic cauda syndrome or neurogenic intermittent claudication (spinal canal stenosis) became unlikely and vascular intermittent claudication likely. The femoral pulses were absent. Angiography showed severe stenosis of the distal aorta which was successfully treated by percutaneous transluminal angioplasty."},
